destination: Litterbox lets a user rewrite the content and history of a sandbox's branch. The sandbox adopts the rewrite when it has no unsaved work. A divergence is handed to the user on the host as a merge based on the revision both sides agreed on. Git and jj are supported to the limits of their capabilities.
created: 2026-11-18
status: open
settled: "Both Git and jj are supported behind one VCS trait; ConflictModel::Blocking; Native and notes rejected/deferred; both VCS hold and stage on the host; explicit `[vcs] tool` with per-VCS configuration sections in the untracked local override, recorded per sandbox; WorkspaceBinding::{Copied, Bound} with no default and legacy Copied; four-tree identity with `Workspace` measured against the sandbox branch; the sandbox adopts when clean and holds the branch when dirty; when the shared branch is conflicted it falls back to `synced` and the user resolves; neither VCS merges in the sandbox and the host resolves in a staging workspace with `synced` as the base; staging is consent-gated and never writes the user's checkout; dropped tips stay in the branch reflog; MCP `workspace` block, `sandbox-status`, `sandbox-reconcile`; operator `status` and `reconcile`; `workspaces`/`synced` refs; jj co-located clone (bookmarks are branches), scoped abort; history is user configuration with no default. Agent-side resolution (T-023) happens in the sandbox's own tree: no second tree, no second sandbox, and no agent write to a host tree; the MCP tools reach only the sandbox; the working copy is ambient and nothing holds it, only the branch advance is held; conflict state is whatever the VCS reports for the revision `/src` holds, so 'resolving' is not a mode; a conflict-observing tool reports paths with hunk and side counts but never the hunks; agent access to history is out of scope and is expected to come from user-provided read-only host access, with an MCP VCS server as a later ADR; edits become snapshots; state changes reach the agent from every mutating operation. Git has one conflict model (an index) and jj two workflows (resolve at the introducing commit, or resolve in a child then squash), and T-024 shows agents pick the first unprompted. Three rulings settle the resolution path: Litterbox performs every host write and the agent writes no ref; `read` serves the three sides of a conflicted path; the resolved check reports a per-side diff and refuses only what the VCS refuses."
next: "Frontier empty. T-023 and T-024 answered; the brief is `maps/2026-11-18-litterbox-vcs-history-sync/brief-agent-side-conflict-resolution.md`. The ADR is at `adrs/2026-11-23-agent-side-conflict-resolution` with spec, plan and tasks rendered. Implementation is 15 of 18 tasks: the conflict primitives, the resolution record and its three actions, materialisation, abandon, the divergence finish with its per-side report, `sandbox-reconcile`, `sandbox-status`, `read` with a side, the workspace block and delete cleanup all land. T-018, resolving a conflicted shared-branch tip, is outstanding, and T-016 and T-017 wait on it, so the spec stays `draft`. One ruling is still open: whether Litterbox may rewrite a commit the user owns to fix a conflicted tip. Remaining outside this map: the Git marker-tree safety net from T-022; the lifecycle delta (Pending, staging cleanup, delete warning); history/undo ([history] no default, Git reflog, jj op log)."
adr: adrs/2026-11-19-decouple-vcs-from-orchestration
adrs: "adrs/2026-11-19-decouple-vcs-from-orchestration; adrs/2026-11-21-hide-vcs-metadata-from-sandboxes; adrs/2026-11-22-adopt-rewritten-branches; adrs/2026-11-22-litterbox-jj-support; adrs/2026-11-23-agent-side-conflict-resolution"
decision: "Git and jj sit behind one VCS trait with opaque revisions and capabilities. Define `vcs` and `vcs::git_common` first, add arch-lint rules, adapt the existing Git implementation to the interface, build the plan surface on Git `Blocking`, and add jj later as a second implementation that uses `vcs::git_common` and never calls `vcs::git`. Git uses `Blocking` as a hold: the shared branch stays at the user tip while the sandbox snapshots its own branch, and the host resolves the difference. jj uses the same hold and host-staging model (one jj workspace per sandbox, co-located clone, `.jj` masked); native conflicted commits and agent-side resolution are part of the goal; T-023 is open, and the fallback is T-021/T-022. `Deferred` and git-notes emulation are rejected. The source state is derived from Base/Workspace/Merge axes over four trees, and `Workspace` compares the workspace with the sandbox branch. One `plan` is the only consumer. No sandbox tool is blocked while diverged; only the branch advance is held. Every sandbox tool returns a proactive `workspace` block; `sandbox-status` and `sandbox-reconcile` are the model surface, and `sandbox-reconcile` stages only. `status` and `reconcile` with `--finish` and `--abort` are the operator surface. Git staging is a linked worktree under the data directory on a temporary branch and jj staging is a jj workspace; the merge uses `synced` as its base because Git's inferred base is wrong after a rebase. The user's checkout is never written. Dropped tips stay in the branch reflog; no new ref is minted. History retention and restore semantics are user configuration with no default; Git uses the reflog, jj uses its op log, and the user's repository is not the history store. Open risks: the masked `.jj` is never absent — an empty mountpoint under bubblewrap/Docker, present-but-inaccessible under Seatbelt's deny rule (T-018); the co-located clone's `.git` is the only Git view; the main workspace never holds a conflicted tree, so the Git consumer gap depends on the conflict living on the shared branch (T-021); aborts must be scoped and clone operations serialised; delete warns about unmerged work and `--force` only means stop it while running."
tickets[24]{id,question,blockers,state,answer,evidence}:
  T-001,"Can a co-located jj clone host one jj workspace per sandbox, and how do its branches reach the user's checkout?",,answered,"Yes: `jj git clone` creates a co-located repo with `.jj` and `.git`; each sandbox is `jj workspace add`; bookmarks are branches in the clone's own `.git`, so the user's checkout fetches and pushes directly with no `jj git export`.",evidence/T-001.md
  T-002,"Can a jj workspace be bind-mounted into a sandbox with `.jj` masked on bubblewrap, Docker and Seatbelt?",,answered,"Bubblewrap and Docker hide the contents of `.jj` with a tmpfs or empty volume; the directory entry remains empty; Seatbelt is unprobed on Linux; a backend that cannot mask must refuse `Bound`.",evidence/T-002.md
  T-003,"How does jj expose conflict paths and their base/ours/theirs sides, and does editing the materialised markers resolve the conflict or is `jj resolve` required?",T-001,answered,"Paths come from `jj resolve --list`; sides from `jj diff --from <base> --to <ours|theirs> --git -- <path>`; editing the materialised markers and snapshotting clears the working-copy conflict, so `jj resolve` is optional (merge-tool entry only).",evidence/T-003.md
  T-004,"Does `git merge-tree --write-tree --merge-base=<tree>` produce a usable conflict tree and exit status, and can that tree be committed and annotated as a conflicted commit?",,answered,"Yes: exit 1 on conflict, the tree OID is the first stdout line, the tree contains markers, and `commit-tree` yields an ordinary two-parent commit with no conflict metadata.",evidence/T-004.md
  T-005,"Can a conflicted Git commit be marked with refs/notes so Litterbox can compute inherited conflict for descendants, and what is the ref/note layout?",T-004,answered,"Technically yes, but no tool ecosystem uses notes for conflict or divergence; git-appraise and Gerrit use notes for review metadata only. Reject the pattern; both VCS use host staging; native conflicts deferred.",evidence/T-005.md
  T-006,"Can `rmcp` tool results carry a structured `workspace` block alongside the existing content, and what is the exact schema?",,answered,"Yes: `CallToolResult.content` is a `Vec<ContentBlock>` and `structured_content` is optional; append `ContentBlock::json(workspace)` or use `structured()`.",evidence/T-006.md
  T-007,"What is the exact `plan`/`decide` sequence for each action (tool, snapshot, rewrite, merge, resolve, abort, repair) and each conflict model?","T-001, T-003, T-005",answered,"One `plan` over Base/Workspace/Merge returns ordered steps; pre-tool and post-tool; no tool is blocked, but the hold model keeps the shared branch at the user tip while the sandbox snapshots its own branch; native conflicts deferred; every tool returns the post-plan `workspace` block. T-019 supersedes the Git merge branch: a dirty sandbox blocks the advance instead of merging, and the host resolves.",evidence/T-007.md
  T-008,"How are the `workspaces`, `synced` and `conflicts` refs represented in jj and Git, and who updates them?","T-001, T-007",answered,"Git: branch plus `refs/litterbox/workspaces` and `synced`; history is a bridge reflog; `Blocking` merge sides in the record; no notes or conflict refs. jj: bookmark (the shared branch), synced as a Git ref, the workspace's working-copy commit as the sandbox branch, and the op log; bookmarks are branches, so no export; abort is scoped, not a global op restore. T-019 fixes the `workspaces` ref as the sandbox branch and the `ours` side; T-020 fixes retention.",evidence/T-008.md
  T-009,"How do pause, missing, repair and delete interact with the source state, especially a conflicted sandbox?",T-007,answered,"Lifecycle and source are orthogonal; pause/resume do not touch the source; repair materialises the branch and re-materialises a `Blocking` merge from record sides; `--force` only means stop it while running, and delete warns about unmerged work against the recorded base.",evidence/T-009.md
  T-010,"What is the migration and configuration path for VCS selection and workspace binding on existing records?",,answered,"`[vcs] tool` with a per-VCS configuration section (`[vcs.git]`, `[vcs.jj]`) mirroring `[backends.*]`; the selected section supplies binding; required at open — a missing selector is an error for every command — and stored per sandbox; legacy records are Git + Copied facts; incompatible combinations are refused at validation, never defaulted. The section is a workspace setting in the untracked `.agentkit/litterbox/config.local.toml`, not machine-wide user configuration: a machine hosts many workspaces, so one user file cannot carry their choices. The committed project file cannot set it.",evidence/T-010.md
  T-011,"What is the operator CLI surface, and how does it share the `plan` logic with MCP?",T-007,answered,"`status`, `conflicts`, `resolve`, `abort`, `shell`; every command calls the same orchestrator `plan`; no `sync` command (reconciliation is lazy); `--json` mirrors the MCP `workspace` block. T-019 supersedes the Git commands: `status` and `reconcile` with `--finish` and `--abort`; the sandbox never resolves.",evidence/T-011.md
  T-012,"How are `.jj`/`.git` masked per backend, and what does the backend do when it cannot mask a `Bound` workspace?",T-002,answered,"Bubblewrap and Docker mask `.jj` contents with a tmpfs or empty volume; the path remains as an empty mountpoint; a backend that cannot mask refuses `Bound`; Seatbelt is unprobed.",evidence/T-002.md
  T-013,"What is the current module layout, where is it Git-coupled, and what has to move for one VCS-neutral trait and a `plan`-driven orchestrator?",,answered,"Sandbox/backends are neutral; `scm` (1,777 lines) holds a Git-shaped trait and `GitScm`; `vcs_store` is a bare-Git store; `orchestrator/service.rs` (2,270) imports `ThreadSafeScm`/`VcsStore`/`ScmMode` and calls `git2::Repository::discover` directly; `domain::ScmError` wraps `git2::Error`; `mcp.rs` (2,276) and `cli.rs` (1,572) are monoliths. Define the VCS interface and `vcs::git_common` first, add arch-lint, adapt Git, build the plan/conflict surface, then add jj.",evidence/T-013.md
  T-014,"Should Git emulate deferred conflict resolution with notes/refs, given the lack of tool adoption?",T-005,answered,"No. Notes are a metadata pattern for review and attribution, not conflict state; both VCS use host staging and native conflicts are deferred. `Deferred` is rejected.",evidence/T-005.md
  T-015,"How should multiple steps of sandbox history be kept without putting bookkeeping refs in the user's repository?",,answered,"Retention and restore semantics are user configuration with no default; Git uses a bridge reflog on `synced`, jj uses its op log; if `[history]` is absent, history/restore report unconfigured. The user's repository is not the history store. T-020: a dropped tip stays in the branch reflog; no new ref is minted.",evidence/T-015.md
  T-016,"What gaps does a co-located jj clone open, and are they acceptable?",,answered,"C is accepted: only the Git remote is external, and this model never creates a conflicted jj commit; native conflicts are deferred. Bookmarks are branches, so there is no export to surface; scope aborts and serialise operations on the shared clone.",evidence/T-016.md
  T-017,"What is the generic VCS interface, what code is shared between backends, and what arch-lint rules keep VCS code out of the orchestration code?",,answered,"Define `vcs` (trait, `Revision`, `TreeId`, `Capabilities`, `Merge`, `Conflict`, `ConflictSides`) and `vcs::git_common` (Git concepts: ignore, archive, object/ref primitives, path safety, naming) first; the contract must also express the operations orchestration uses today (branch create/delete, branch enumeration, archive, snapshot commit, snapshot filter, remote/direct fact); adapt Git; add jj later. Orchestrator imports `vcs` only (never `vcs::git_common`); `vcs::git` and `vcs::jj` may import `vcs`/`vcs::git_common` but never each other; arch-lint 0.11.0 scopes with `deny-scope-dep` and `independent` enforce the boundary.",evidence/T-017.md
  T-018,"Can Seatbelt deny a path inside a workspace it already allows, so a bound workspace can hide `.git` or `.jj`?",,answered,"Yes: SBPL resolves rules last-match-wins, so `(deny file-read* (subpath <ws>/.jj))` and `(deny file-write* (subpath <ws>/.jj))` emitted after the workspace allow rules override them; the `.jj` entry stays visible in a listing but every read, write and stat under it returns EPERM, and descendants inherit it.",evidence/T-018.md
  T-019,"Where does a rewritten branch get reconciled, and what does the sandbox do?",,answered,"The sandbox never merges. It adopts when its workspace matches its own branch and holds the shared branch at the user tip when it does not. The host resolves in a staging workspace under the data directory (a linked worktree for Git, a jj workspace for jj). The merge uses the synced revision as its base because Git's inferred base is wrong after a rebase. Staging is a command the user runs or an MCP tool the harness may allow; it never writes the user's checkout. Finish fast-forwards when the branch is free and prints the command when the checkout holds it.",evidence/T-019.md
  T-020,"How are sandbox revisions that a rewrite drops retained?",,answered,"The Git branch reflog; jj uses its operation log. Adoption reports the old tip once and names the reflog; the object stays reachable for the reflog lifetime. No new ref and no record field. The bare store logs every ref update; under direct the user's repository logs branches by default.",evidence/T-020.md
  T-021,"When the shared branch is a conflicted revision, does the sandbox fall back to the last non-conflicted history, and at which points does it check?",,answered,"Yes: on every operation that reads source state, check the shared branch; if conflicted, hold at synced and the sandbox branch and report; adopt only a non-conflicted tip. The main workspace stays buildable.",evidence/T-021.md
  T-022,"How does each VCS report a conflicted tip (jj commit state, Git marker tree), and what does the MCP workspace block say?",T-021,answered,"jj: `jj resolve --list -r <rev>` and the `conflict` template; Git: a marker scan of the tip tree, as a safety net behind the host staging. The workspace block reports `held`, `reason: shared branch is conflicted`, synced, branch, paths and the next step.",evidence/T-022.md
  T-023,"Can an agent resolve a deferred conflict, and if so what resolution context and knowledge does it need?","T-021, T-024",answered,"Yes, in the sandbox's own tree and never a second tree or second sandbox: the conflicted revision is materialised as the working copy, the VCS reports paths with a hunk count and a side count, abandon re-materialises the merge from its recorded parents, and the agent's writes land as ordinary snapshots. Resolved is the VCS conflict report; whether that is enough is open, because T-024 produced trees that reported clean and had destroyed content.",evidence/T-023/README.md
  T-024,"Which jj workflow do agents understand: resolving at the introducing commit, or resolving in a child and squashing into it?",,answered,"Neither framing changes the procedure: both agents resolved in place at the introducing commit and neither added a child commit, so resolving in a child and squashing it adds work without adding capability. What they lack is verification, since two runs produced trees that passed every conflict, marker and ancestry check while dropping one side entirely and leaving conflict-header text as content, and recovery, where the first instinct was a repository-wide `jj op restore` that would rewind every other sandbox in the shared clone.",evidence/T-024.md
